WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:03.603
-Our next guest is
a talented actor and comedian

00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:06.005
you know from his work on
"The Daily Show" and "Evil."

00:00:06.005 --> 00:00:07.973
His new panel show,
"Would I Lie to You?",

00:00:07.973 --> 00:00:09.542
airs Saturdays on The CW.

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:10.676
Let's take a look.

00:00:10.676 --> 00:00:12.178
-If this is a lie, you didn't

00:00:12.178 --> 00:00:13.779
need to do the horse-meat bit.

00:00:13.779 --> 00:00:15.347
Like, we didn't need that.

00:00:15.347 --> 00:00:17.450
-Do you mind if I talk to
my client for a second?

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:18.484
[ Laughter ]

00:00:18.484 --> 00:00:19.752
-Sure.
-Off mic.

00:00:19.752 --> 00:00:22.254
-I'm getting hot.
-I would say...

00:00:22.254 --> 00:00:24.724
-I suppose a falcon or...

00:00:24.724 --> 00:00:27.159
-Well, horse meat
is a brand of bird seed.

00:00:27.159 --> 00:00:29.195
-No! See? See?

00:00:29.195 --> 00:00:34.868
Brooke Shields, if this is true,
you win the entire show.

00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:36.135
-Yeah.

00:00:36.135 --> 00:00:39.572
-Please welcome back to the show
Aasif Mandvi, everybody!

00:00:39.572 --> 00:00:42.442
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:53.152 --> 00:00:54.320
Welcome back to the show!

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:56.255
-Thank you.
Thanks for having me back.

00:00:56.255 --> 00:00:57.524
-It a long time --
We were talking backstage.

00:00:57.524 --> 00:00:58.791
A long time
since you've been here.

00:00:58.791 --> 00:01:02.094
And the last couple of years,
giant life change.

00:01:02.094 --> 00:01:04.697
-Giant. Giant.
Well, for all of us.

00:01:04.697 --> 00:01:06.432
Oh, you mean my baby?

00:01:06.432 --> 00:01:07.767
-Yeah, you had a baby.
-I had a child.

00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:09.735
-Yeah. We all had your child.

00:01:09.735 --> 00:01:11.371
-All I think about
is the pandemic.

00:01:11.371 --> 00:01:13.740
-Yeah. That's very exciting.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:16.208
So, how old? Boy, girl?

00:01:16.208 --> 00:01:18.011
-Boy. A boy.
-Okay. Yeah.

00:01:18.011 --> 00:01:21.647
-And he's 2 years old.
He just turned 2.

00:01:21.647 --> 00:01:24.617
He was born on March 14th,

00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:26.986
like three days before
New York went into lockdown.

00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:29.389
-Yeah, that's -- Somebody loved
the lockdown.

00:01:29.389 --> 00:01:30.590
-Exactly.
-"Whoo!"

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:33.559
-"Lockdown!"
-Is your birthday March 14th?

00:01:33.559 --> 00:01:34.760
-Whoo!
-Whoo!

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:36.195
-Yeah. Great. Okay.

00:01:36.195 --> 00:01:37.497
-They're very -- they're not --

00:01:37.497 --> 00:01:39.899
They're not
the best communicators, yeah.

00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:42.268
Also, like, like, really
lukewarm on their birthday.

00:01:42.268 --> 00:01:43.536
-Yeah.

00:01:43.536 --> 00:01:45.471
Yeah, I've had some good ones
and some bad ones.

00:01:45.471 --> 00:01:47.306
So, you have a 2-year-old.

00:01:47.306 --> 00:01:50.009
This is -- I feel like
personalities are in bloom now?

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:53.178
-Oh, my gosh.
He -- Yeah, I mean, he's great.

00:01:53.178 --> 00:01:56.315
You know, I waited till
I was older to have a child,

00:01:56.315 --> 00:01:57.650
and so it's great, 'cause I get
to really be with him

00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:58.951
and spend time with him.

00:01:58.951 --> 00:02:03.255
And the other day, I went into
his room in the morning,

00:02:03.255 --> 00:02:06.726
and he sat up
and he went, "Poop!"

00:02:06.726 --> 00:02:08.628
And I was like, "Oh, great," you
know, "You've done a poop?

00:02:08.628 --> 00:02:09.896
That's great
you're communicating

00:02:09.896 --> 00:02:11.297
and telling me that you pooped."

00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:13.800
I put him on the changing table,
I open his diaper, nothing.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.802
And I said, "Ishan,
you didn't do a poop."

00:02:16.802 --> 00:02:19.105
And he looks at me
and he goes, "Joking you."

00:02:19.105 --> 00:02:20.874
-Oh. Jackpot.

00:02:20.874 --> 00:02:23.342
-I was like --
-Jackpot.

00:02:23.342 --> 00:02:27.680
-I was like, "That's solid.
That is a solid joke."

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:30.383
-Yeah, like, level one.
That's a good level-one joke.

00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:31.784
-Absolutely. I mean,
we can get into the whole

00:02:31.784 --> 00:02:35.354
"lying is not joking"
kind of thing later,

00:02:35.354 --> 00:02:37.323
but for right now,
they're solid.

00:02:37.323 --> 00:02:39.292
He did a thing the other day
where he's now learned the song,

00:02:39.292 --> 00:02:40.393
you know...

00:02:40.393 --> 00:02:42.728
♪ Red and yellow
and pink and blue ♪

00:02:42.728 --> 00:02:45.898
So, he was singing that
and then he just looks at me

00:02:45.898 --> 00:02:48.901
and he goes, "Now funny way."

00:02:48.901 --> 00:02:50.036
And I was like, "Okay."

00:02:50.036 --> 00:02:51.037
And he just started going...

00:02:51.037 --> 00:02:53.773
♪ Red and yellow and pink...♪

00:02:53.773 --> 00:02:56.542
And I was like,
"Again, solid joke."

00:02:56.542 --> 00:02:57.910
-I love it.

00:02:57.910 --> 00:03:00.079
It's like he's, like, working on
a lounge-singer act, yeah.

00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:01.848
-He's got a
whole -- By the time he's 3,

00:03:01.848 --> 00:03:02.882
he's going on the road.

00:03:02.882 --> 00:03:04.017
-This is very nice.

00:03:04.017 --> 00:03:06.218
So, I also started later
as a dad,

00:03:06.218 --> 00:03:08.554
and I think it's true,
like, the great thing is,

00:03:08.554 --> 00:03:10.923
there's nothing in your life --
Like, no one --

00:03:10.923 --> 00:03:12.558
if you didn't have a kid,
no one would want to

00:03:12.558 --> 00:03:13.659
hang out with you anyway.

00:03:13.659 --> 00:03:15.027
-Right, right.
-'Cause you're too old.

00:03:15.027 --> 00:03:16.495
-Yes, exactly.

00:03:16.495 --> 00:03:18.597
And I don't go out any -- Well,
we had enjoyed the pandemic,

00:03:18.597 --> 00:03:21.267
so it was actually
kind of weirdly great.

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:24.970
Like, we were sort of in
a bubble of having a child,

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:27.606
and the whole world was
in a bubble, you know?

00:03:27.606 --> 00:03:31.444
And so we didn't have any FOMO
or any of that stuff.

00:03:31.444 --> 00:03:35.147
But it's been really amazing,
'cause I've just gotten to spend

00:03:35.147 --> 00:03:36.749
a lot of really
quality time with him.

00:03:36.749 --> 00:03:37.883
-That's excellent.

00:03:37.883 --> 00:03:40.586
Yeah, we have to find
silver linings.

00:03:40.586 --> 00:03:43.222
So, this show that you have on
The CW right now --

00:03:43.222 --> 00:03:44.890
this is based on a British show.

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:45.991
-It's based on
a British show, yes.

00:03:45.991 --> 00:03:48.828
-And when did you
discover this show?

00:03:48.828 --> 00:03:55.301
-So, this was -- 2019, I started
watching this British --

00:03:55.301 --> 00:03:57.704
I'm obsessed with,
like, British shows,

00:03:57.704 --> 00:03:58.704
'cause I grew up in England.

00:03:58.704 --> 00:04:00.472
So I watch BritBox.

00:04:00.472 --> 00:04:01.541
Like, I'm an addict.

00:04:01.541 --> 00:04:02.842
-Yep, I love
a good BritBox show.

00:04:02.842 --> 00:04:04.877
-I watched this show called
"Would I Lie to You?"

00:04:04.877 --> 00:04:08.213
on the British -- BritBox,
and I fell in love with it.

00:04:08.213 --> 00:04:10.016
And I started --
And my wife and I would watch it

00:04:10.016 --> 00:04:11.350
at the end of the day,
because it was

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:13.586
during the Trump administration,
and, frankly, you know,

00:04:13.586 --> 00:04:14.720
we needed something
to get us through it.

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:16.021
-Yeah. Like, at some point,
you're like,

00:04:16.021 --> 00:04:17.590
"Let's watch British celebrities
lie to each other," yeah.

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:19.425
-So it was just a way to laugh

00:04:19.425 --> 00:04:20.793
and have fun
at the end of the night.

00:04:20.793 --> 00:04:23.996
And so I actually told
Robert King,

00:04:23.996 --> 00:04:27.333
who is the creator and executive
producer of the show "Evil"

00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:28.567
that I'm also on --

00:04:28.567 --> 00:04:31.504
I told him about it,
and he started watching it

00:04:31.504 --> 00:04:33.939
and fell in love with it
and then came to me and said,

00:04:33.939 --> 00:04:36.542
"Why don't we do an
American version of this show."

00:04:36.542 --> 00:04:39.445
And, you know, as an Indian,
person of Indian descent,

00:04:39.445 --> 00:04:41.179
I am more than happy to
steal something from

00:04:41.179 --> 00:04:42.682
the British anytime...

00:04:42.682 --> 00:04:44.249
-It's time to, like,
even the score a little bit.

00:04:44.249 --> 00:04:45.551
-...that I can.

00:04:45.551 --> 00:04:47.120
I'm like, "I'll take that."
-You're owed a couple.

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:49.221
-I am. I am.

00:04:49.221 --> 00:04:50.523
-But that's interesting,
though, like,

00:04:50.523 --> 00:04:52.357
you bringing a British
sensibility here,

00:04:52.357 --> 00:04:53.493
because you mentioned
you grew up in England.

00:04:53.493 --> 00:04:54.494
-Yeah.

00:04:54.494 --> 00:04:55.995
-And then you --
How old were you?

00:04:55.995 --> 00:04:57.230
You moved to Florida, right?

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:59.832
-I moved to Tampa, Florida,
when I was 16.

00:04:59.832 --> 00:05:01.033
-16.
-Yes.

00:05:01.033 --> 00:05:04.036
Yeah, anybody, anybody?
No, not one person.

00:05:04.036 --> 00:05:05.704
-And you were sort of like --
I would imagine, like,

00:05:05.704 --> 00:05:10.442
Northern England to Florida
is a real leap.

00:05:10.442 --> 00:05:12.444
-Really? You think so?
-Yeah.

00:05:12.444 --> 00:05:14.080
A good stylistic leap.

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:15.681
And at 16, like, you're not --

00:05:15.681 --> 00:05:17.149
I mean, you're kind of who
you are at that point, like...

00:05:17.149 --> 00:05:19.018
-Yeah. Well, yeah.

00:05:19.018 --> 00:05:23.956
I went from a coal-mining,
Northern, gray English town

00:05:23.956 --> 00:05:27.893
to Tampa, Florida,
which, you know --

00:05:27.893 --> 00:05:31.063
And it was interesting,
because when I got to America,

00:05:31.063 --> 00:05:33.032
I often joked that,
like, Americans think about

00:05:33.032 --> 00:05:34.233
the rest of the world the way

00:05:34.233 --> 00:05:36.201
New Yorkers think about
the rest of America.

00:05:36.201 --> 00:05:39.204
-Yeah.
-Like, they don't, you know?

00:05:39.204 --> 00:05:42.408
And, so, I got here, and people
didn't really know what I was.

00:05:42.408 --> 00:05:43.842
They thought I was Australian.

00:05:43.842 --> 00:05:45.744
They thought I was,
you know, Lebanese.

00:05:45.744 --> 00:05:47.180
They didn't know.

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.615
So, but it was funny, because
when I first got to America,

00:05:49.615 --> 00:05:52.551
I was thrown into
this American high school,

00:05:52.551 --> 00:05:54.854
and I was just, you know,
this little English boy

00:05:54.854 --> 00:05:57.422
in an all-American
sort of high school.

00:05:57.422 --> 00:06:01.493
And I just remember I made this
friend after a couple of days

00:06:01.493 --> 00:06:02.762
in high school.

00:06:02.762 --> 00:06:04.497
It was, like, not really --
I've never spoken to

00:06:04.497 --> 00:06:06.999
the guy again,
but it was two days --

00:06:06.999 --> 00:06:08.867
He was my friend after two days.

00:06:08.867 --> 00:06:11.871
And while I was talking to him,
he suddenly said,

00:06:11.871 --> 00:06:13.372
"Okay, well,
I'm gonna take off."

00:06:13.372 --> 00:06:15.874
And I remember, like, my little
English brain was just like,

00:06:15.874 --> 00:06:17.143
"You're going to take off?

00:06:17.143 --> 00:06:18.444
What do you mean?"

00:06:18.444 --> 00:06:20.779
And I just could not fathom
this or understand it.

00:06:20.779 --> 00:06:22.881
We had a teacher who
said to me -- And this must --

00:06:22.881 --> 00:06:24.283
-Did you think he was gonna
have, like, a jet pack?

00:06:24.283 --> 00:06:27.319
-I thought he was gonna
literally take off right there.

00:06:27.319 --> 00:06:28.387
Like, I was like, "Do you mean

00:06:28.387 --> 00:06:30.622
you're gonna take off
right now?"

00:06:30.622 --> 00:06:33.226
But -- And then we had
a teacher who --

00:06:33.226 --> 00:06:34.493
And this must be
a Southern thing.

00:06:34.493 --> 00:06:35.962
I don't know if anybody here
is from the South.

00:06:35.962 --> 00:06:39.264
But she wanted me to take my pen
and put it up,

00:06:39.264 --> 00:06:40.800
which means put it away.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:42.735
It's a Southern vernacular.
-Okay, gotcha.

00:06:42.735 --> 00:06:46.872
-And she said that to me,
and I literally took the pen

00:06:46.872 --> 00:06:51.310
and I didn't know what --
and I just put it above my head.

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:53.980
And she sent me to detention.

00:06:55.281 --> 00:06:56.415
-Yeah, it's funny.

00:06:56.415 --> 00:06:58.116
Like, that's a tough --
Like, 16's tough,

00:06:58.116 --> 00:06:59.185
'cause, like, it's not --

00:06:59.185 --> 00:07:01.821
You're not, like,
a charming British scamp.

00:07:01.821 --> 00:07:03.889
It's like,
"Who's this 16-year-old..."

00:07:03.889 --> 00:07:06.592
-Yeah, I tried to work the
English accent for a while.

00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:08.361
-Did people ask you
things about England?

00:07:08.361 --> 00:07:11.363
What did people from Tampa think
an English person would know?

00:07:11.363 --> 00:07:13.833
-Well, they thought
I knew The Beatles.

00:07:13.833 --> 00:07:17.370
Like, it was -- You know, it was
the '80s, and I think they --

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:19.205
They were just like,
"Have you ever met any of them?"

00:07:19.205 --> 00:07:21.140
And I'm like,
"Yes, that's right. I have."

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:22.841
And I was just trying to
work the English angle

00:07:22.841 --> 00:07:24.110
for a little while.

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:26.278
I think there were a couple of
girls in my school

00:07:26.278 --> 00:07:28.514
who might have been Anglophiles
who appreciated it.

00:07:28.514 --> 00:07:30.049
-And then they were like --
Probably by day three,

00:07:30.049 --> 00:07:32.384
they were like, "I don't think
this guy knows The Beatles.

00:07:32.384 --> 00:07:33.619
I don't think
he's personally met them."

00:07:33.619 --> 00:07:36.255
-I think my lack of ability
with ladies, like,

00:07:36.255 --> 00:07:38.024
outweighed the fact
that I had an English accent.

00:07:38.024 --> 00:07:39.491
-Yeah, they were like,
"I think that guy's

00:07:39.491 --> 00:07:40.859
close personal friends with
Paul McCartney,

00:07:40.859 --> 00:07:43.029
but it's still not worth it."

00:07:43.029 --> 00:07:45.832
You -- So, you --
Did you bring --

00:07:45.832 --> 00:07:48.501
Like, when you think back
to, like, your --

00:07:48.501 --> 00:07:50.269
you know, for me,
you know, I was lucky,

00:07:50.269 --> 00:07:52.038
'cause my parents loved,
like, Monty Python.

00:07:52.038 --> 00:07:53.906
I feel like my education was,
like, "SNL."

00:07:53.906 --> 00:07:57.376
Was yours the Monty Python
era of British comedy?

00:07:57.376 --> 00:08:01.647
-I grew up with Monty Python and
I grew up with "Fawlty Towers"

00:08:01.647 --> 00:08:03.082
and all those British sitcoms.

00:08:03.082 --> 00:08:04.283
Like, you know,
there was one called

00:08:04.283 --> 00:08:05.451
"Not the Nine O'Clock News,"

00:08:05.451 --> 00:08:07.086
which Rowan Atkinson
came out of.

00:08:07.086 --> 00:08:10.455
So I sort of grew up
with all of that in my head.

00:08:10.455 --> 00:08:13.525
And, in fact, when I was a
little kid, I made this, like,

00:08:13.525 --> 00:08:16.328
cassette tape that was --

00:08:16.328 --> 00:08:18.330
You know, it was back when
you had cassettes,

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:20.232
and you would just talk into
the little tape player.

00:08:20.232 --> 00:08:24.637
And I made a tape called
"The Giant Man-Eating Ostrich."

00:08:24.637 --> 00:08:29.074
And it was four and a half hours
long of me, as a 10-year-old,

00:08:29.074 --> 00:08:31.911
just doing all the characters.

00:08:31.911 --> 00:08:33.912
And it was very
Monty Python-esque,

00:08:33.912 --> 00:08:35.581
where this ostrich
was just, like,

00:08:35.581 --> 00:08:38.584
stomping on all the people in
this village, in this town.

00:08:38.584 --> 00:08:41.086
And then my parents
would play it for guests

00:08:41.086 --> 00:08:42.254
when they would come over.

00:08:42.254 --> 00:08:43.922
-Yeah. When they would
come over for, like,

00:08:43.922 --> 00:08:45.091
long dinner parties?

00:08:45.091 --> 00:08:46.925
-Yeah, and my dad would be like,
"Look at our son.

00:08:46.925 --> 00:08:48.227
Look how talented he is."

00:08:48.227 --> 00:08:49.695
And he'd put this thing in,
and you could just see

00:08:49.695 --> 00:08:52.130
people's eyes start to water,
because they had to listen to

00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:54.733
four and a half hours of some
little English boy going,

00:08:54.733 --> 00:08:56.535
"There's a giant
eating ostrich coming.

00:08:56.535 --> 00:08:57.669
Oh, mummy!"

00:08:57.669 --> 00:09:00.906
And just...
-That's a very proud father.

00:09:00.906 --> 00:09:02.107
That's what I'm gonna
take away from this story

00:09:02.107 --> 00:09:03.175
is that you have
a very proud father.

00:09:03.175 --> 00:09:04.343
-He was very proud of me.

00:09:04.343 --> 00:09:06.812
-So, but you actually --
You ruined dinner parties

00:09:06.812 --> 00:09:09.314
for years after,
because you actually --

00:09:09.314 --> 00:09:11.750
You and Connie Britton,
the wonderful actor --

00:09:11.750 --> 00:09:12.751
And I shouldn't say "ruined."

00:09:12.751 --> 00:09:13.819
I was being facetious.

00:09:13.819 --> 00:09:15.821
-Well, we ruined a few.
We ruined a few.

00:09:15.821 --> 00:09:17.956
-You were professional --
You're young actors in New York.

00:09:17.956 --> 00:09:19.191
-Mm-hmm. Yeah.

00:09:19.191 --> 00:09:21.527
-You would work
murder-mystery dinner parties?

00:09:21.527 --> 00:09:24.796
-Murder-mystery dinner parties.
-And so you would go as actors.

00:09:24.796 --> 00:09:28.434
-Yes. It was when I first got to
New York and I needed a job,

00:09:28.434 --> 00:09:31.036
and they were paying $50 a show.

00:09:31.036 --> 00:09:33.205
you know, which was
a solid rate, good rate.

00:09:33.205 --> 00:09:35.340
And we'd go out
to a party somewhere

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:38.477
in Jersey or Staten Island or
Long Island somewhere.

00:09:38.477 --> 00:09:40.612
And you'd just
sort of pretend like

00:09:40.612 --> 00:09:42.448
you were a guest at the party.

00:09:42.448 --> 00:09:44.850
And then a hit man would come in
and kill someone.

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:47.386
And then there would
be a detective,

00:09:47.386 --> 00:09:48.788
and then the whole thing
would turn into

00:09:48.788 --> 00:09:51.523
a murder mystery,
and people would play along.

00:09:51.523 --> 00:09:54.093
And that's where I met Connie.

00:09:54.093 --> 00:09:57.829
And she and I used to do that
together and make the money.

00:09:57.829 --> 00:10:02.034
And there was one -- I don't --
This was a legendary one

00:10:02.034 --> 00:10:05.237
where, apparently, they did a
murder mystery in Staten Island,

00:10:05.237 --> 00:10:07.406
and I'll just say there were
a lot of guys there named Tony.

00:10:07.406 --> 00:10:08.807
-Okay, lot of Tonys, yeah.

00:10:08.807 --> 00:10:12.244
-A lot of Tonys, and it was a
guy named Tony's birthday.

00:10:12.244 --> 00:10:14.713
And it was
in an Italian restaurant,

00:10:14.713 --> 00:10:17.049
and there was some poor actor

00:10:17.049 --> 00:10:19.818
who had been hired to be
the hit man

00:10:19.818 --> 00:10:23.823
to come in and kill --
"kill" -- another actor

00:10:23.823 --> 00:10:27.259
who was hired as the guy
who was gonna get killed.

00:10:27.259 --> 00:10:31.029
So, this young actor walks in,
pulls out a gun,

00:10:31.029 --> 00:10:33.565
and asks for
the actor who he's gonna shoot,

00:10:33.565 --> 00:10:36.402
and 10 guys pulled out pieces.

00:10:36.402 --> 00:10:39.538
And everybody hit the deck.

00:10:39.538 --> 00:10:43.975
And the actor just urinated
all over himself.

00:10:43.975 --> 00:10:46.912
Just shook, dropped the gun,
walked out,

00:10:46.912 --> 00:10:48.580
and left the business, I think.

00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:51.383
Just never was
seen of again, you know?

00:10:51.383 --> 00:10:55.220
-They did give -- They gave him
$55 for that, yeah.

00:10:55.220 --> 00:10:58.023
They gave him a little extra
for danger pay.

00:10:58.023 --> 00:11:03.295
-And then we worked a singles
party for Orthodox Jews.

00:11:03.295 --> 00:11:04.429
That was another one.

00:11:04.429 --> 00:11:06.198
And I did that one
with Connie, actually.

00:11:06.198 --> 00:11:09.201
She and I was there.

00:11:09.201 --> 00:11:13.105
And I said, "How do I pretend to
be an Orthodox Jewish single?"

00:11:13.105 --> 00:11:14.473
And they said,
"You're Sephardic.

00:11:14.473 --> 00:11:15.741
Just don't worry about it."

00:11:15.741 --> 00:11:17.409
And they just stuck
a yarmulke on my head

00:11:17.409 --> 00:11:18.744
and had me go out there.

00:11:18.744 --> 00:11:21.514
And I was telling --
"Just do an Egyptian accent!"

00:11:21.514 --> 00:11:24.749
And then Connie
was playing this very --

00:11:24.749 --> 00:11:26.118
You know,
she was Connie Britton,

00:11:26.118 --> 00:11:29.955
so she was one of the most
attractive people at this party.

00:11:29.955 --> 00:11:31.923
And this poor guy in a yellow --

00:11:31.923 --> 00:11:34.226
I remember he
was wearing a yellow suit,

00:11:34.226 --> 00:11:37.429
and he was talking
to her all night long

00:11:37.429 --> 00:11:39.932
and thought that
he had struck gold.

00:11:39.932 --> 00:11:43.101
-And then about, you know,
an hour into the party,

00:11:43.101 --> 00:11:46.505
like, she dies.

00:11:46.505 --> 00:11:49.441
And I've never seen a man --

00:11:49.441 --> 00:11:52.044
Just, like,
his face just collapsed.

00:11:52.044 --> 00:11:55.414
But I said, "$1,000 a plate."
-That's fantastic.

00:11:55.414 --> 00:11:57.316
You know, that's the thing,
where if you're doing

00:11:57.316 --> 00:11:59.651
a murder-mystery party,
prepare your guests.

00:11:59.651 --> 00:12:01.754
Prepare your guests, 'cause you
could ruin their evening.

00:12:01.754 --> 00:12:03.755
Hey, congratulations on
the new show.

00:12:03.755 --> 00:12:05.390
Congratulations on "Evil,"
as well.

00:12:05.390 --> 00:12:06.692
I know you've got
Season 3 coming out.

00:12:06.692 --> 00:12:07.793
And it's always
so great to see you.

00:12:07.793 --> 00:12:09.661
Guys, Aasif Mandvi.

00:12:09.661 --> 00:12:12.732
"Would I Lie to You?"
airs Saturdays on The CW.

